# Code Architecture Standards

## File Size

- Keep files focused — prefer small, single-purpose modules; most files stay well under ~300 lines. Large domains are split into focused files rather than left as monoliths. A handful of registry/data and dashboard files run longer by design.
- Components: one component per file — split when a file renders more than one distinct section.
- Server Actions: group by domain (e.g., `actions/quotes.ts`, `actions/events.ts`), split large domains into focused files.
- Queries: one file per entity (e.g., `queries/events.ts`, `queries/quotes.ts`).

## Naming Conventions

- Files: `kebab-case.ts` for utilities, `PascalCase.tsx` for components
- Directories: `kebab-case/`
- Functions: `camelCase` — verbs first (e.g., `getEventById`, `createQuoteFromIntake`)
- Types/Interfaces: `PascalCase` — nouns (e.g., `Quote`, `LocationTier`)
- Constants: `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` (e.g., `INTERNAL_ROLES`, `STAGE_CONFIG`)

## Directory Structure

```
src/
  app/              → Pages and route handlers only. No business logic.
  components/
    ui/             → shadcn/ui primitives (never modify directly)
    layout/         → App shell, sidebar, headers
    [domain]/       → Domain-specific components (events/, studio/, catalog/)
  lib/
    queries/        → Supabase read queries, one file per entity
    validations/    → Zod schemas, one file per domain
    utils.ts        → cn() and shared utilities
    roles.ts        → Role/permission helpers
  types/            → TypeScript types and enums
  app/actions/      → Server Actions grouped by domain
```

## Separation of Concerns

- Pages (`page.tsx`) only fetch data and compose components. No business logic in pages.
- Components handle UI rendering and user interaction. No direct Supabase calls in components.
- Server Actions handle mutations. One action per function, clearly named.
- Queries handle reads. Pure functions that return typed data.
- Validations define Zod schemas. Imported by both Server Actions and client forms.
